WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.590
So here, this is a really quite unusual sample
from Goodfellow and you

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:06.060
see this is samarium, so this is one of the
lanthanides.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.440
Ok so samarium is a beautiful element to work
with as it can change

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.000
what we refer to as oxidation states very
easily which gives it many

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.450
more things it can do in chemistry. It’s
also a fascinating element in the

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:23.259
sense that we can use it to date samples from
the moon.

00:00:23.259 --> 00:00:26.760
So this is a sample of samarium and there’s
is a very small amount of it

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.890
here, perhaps half of a gram. But you know
there's some interesting

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.090
things we can see about this package because
we can see on this label it

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.820
says “packed in argon – handle with care.”
Now it’s packed in argon

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.420
because argon is very, very inert, it doesn’t
react with anything, so it’s

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.260
used to store, safely, this sample so that
we can get it to carry out

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.050
research. But I think that this sample has
perhaps become damaged

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:53.170
because, I think, the packets maybe broken
because the samarium is

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:57.400
gone and all you can see there inside is this
white powder which, I

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.230
suspect, is samarium oxide, so unfortunately
not elemental samarium,

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.230
but close.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:07.090
Samarium is also very important because it’s
a neutron absorber,

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:10.600
which means it’s got a lot of use in nuclear
power stations. Basically to

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:11.829
stop them going ‘boom’.

